serial: 8250: service the transmitter of a polled port from the LSR

A port without an interrupt is serviced by serial8250_timeout(), which
hands the IIR to the normal interrupt handler. serial8250_handle_irq()
returns early when the IIR says no interrupt is pending, so a UART that
never reports THRE in its IIR never gets its TX FIFO refilled: the tty
layer queues data, and nothing reaches the wire.

The Intel CE5300's XScale UARTs are such UARTs (Intel documents the same
erratum for the CE4100, see 8250_ce4100.c). openHC runs them polled
because the device tree cannot name their interrupt on this ACPI
platform, and the IO-MCU on ttyS1 then never sees a byte.

serial8250_backup_timeout() already handles UARTs that do not report
THRE reliably, by checking the LSR. Do the same in the polled path: when
THRI is enabled and the LSR says the transmitter is empty, transmit.
A UART whose IIR does report THRE has already been serviced by then, so
this only adds an LSR read per poll.

Replaces the earlier 0010, which special-cased polled XScale ports in
serial8250_handle_irq().

Author: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_core.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_core.c
@@ -209,9 +209,23 @@
 static void serial8250_timeout(struct timer_list *t)
 {
 	struct uart_8250_port *up = timer_container_of(up, t, timer);
+	struct uart_port *port = &up->port;
 
-	up->port.handle_irq(&up->port);
-	mod_timer(&up->timer, jiffies + uart_poll_timeout(&up->port));
+	port->handle_irq(port);
+
+	/*
+	 * A polled port has no interrupt to lose, so do not rely on the IIR
+	 * to report THRE: some UARTs never set it there. Service the
+	 * transmitter from the LSR, as serial8250_backup_timeout() does.
+	 */
+	scoped_guard(serial8250_rpm, up)
+	scoped_guard(uart_port_lock_irqsave, port) {
+		if ((up->ier & UART_IER_THRI) &&
+		    (serial_lsr_in(up) & UART_LSR_THRE))
+			serial8250_tx_chars(up);
+	}
+
+	mod_timer(&up->timer, jiffies + uart_poll_timeout(port));
 }
 
 static void serial8250_backup_timeout(struct timer_list *t)
